":"server","feedback_revision":"f510fde85b5065132b228143"},{"id":"la_batch21_polynomial_greatest_common_divisor_003","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"In the Euclidean algorithm for polynomials, what pair replaces the current pair after division?","options":["The quotient and the dividend","The roots and the field","The divisor and the remainder","The two leading coefficients"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"087fef86b2561c56d9e8a5f4"},{"id":"la_batch21_polynomial_greatest_common_divisor_004","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"When does the Euclidean algorithm stop?","options":["When both polynomials have the same leading coefficient","When a root is found","When the remainder becomes zero","When the quotient has degree one"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"f008969e0a070bd583ba76d7"},{"id":"la_batch21_polynomial_greatest_common_divisor_005","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why is the GCD often normalized to be monic?","options":["To make its degree larger","To change the roots of the input polynomials.","To turn every GCD into a constant","To choose a standard representative among scalar multiples"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"068912298ef6b923775e68fc"},{"id":"la_batch21_polynomial_greatest_common_divisor_006","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"What is the GCD of $x^2-1$ and $x-1$?","options":["$x+1$","$x^2-1$","$1$ only","$x-1$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"e97855fa1524a4489f33bd60"},{"id":"la_batch21_polynomial_greatest_common_divisor_007","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Which polynomial divides both $x^2-4$ and $x-2$?","options":["$x^2+4$","$x+2$","$x-2$","$x$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"902417a2f3ed351cc37f1608"},{"id":"la_batch21_polynomial_greatest_common_divisor_008","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"What is the monic GCD of $2x-2$ and $x-1$ over a field of characteristic not $2$?","options":["$2x-2$","$x-1$","$x+1$","$2$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"8a1abc6151640d2a94a8fa01"},{"id":"la_batch21_polynomial_greatest_common_divisor_009","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"If the Euclidean algorithm ends with last nonzero remainder $x+3$, what is a GCD?","options":["The next quotient","$x+3$","The original dividend only","$0$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"306438e6932e311e9e3b2b51"},{"id":"la_batch21_polynomial_greatest_common_divisor_010","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Which pair clearly has GCD $1$ over $\\mathbb R[x]$?","options":["$x^2$ and $x$","$x^2-1$ and $x-1$","$x^3$ and $x^2$","$x$ and $x+1$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"92515af31a2da98969959602"},{"id":"la_batch21_polynomial_greatest_common_divisor_011","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why are polynomial GCDs not literally unique unless a normalization is chosen?","options":["Any nonzero scalar multiple of a GCD divides the same polynomials","Every pair of polynomials has infinitely many roots","There is no concept of degree for polynomials","The Euclidean algorithm gives unrelated answers each time"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"a1a5bd7c569aea97f34c2082"},{"id":"la_batch21_polynomial_greatest_common_divisor_012","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why does the Euclidean algorithm for polynomial GCDs depend on polynomial long division?","options":["It requires computing determinants","Each step uses quotient and remainder to reduce degree","It works only by listing all roots","It replaces polynomials by their eigenvalues."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"e42316d68a47e964d60b260d"},{"id":"la_batch21_polynomial_greatest_common_divisor_013","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What does it mean for two polynomials to be relatively prime?","options":["They have the same leading coefficient","Their product is zero","Their monic GCD is $1$","They both have degree at least one"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"87ee2356d193723ce70fee3d"},{"id":"la_batch21_polynomial_greatest_common_divisor_014","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Find the monic GCD of $x^2-9$ and $x^2-3x$.","options":["$x-3$","$x^2-9$","$x+3$","$x$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"5d9d0f1c6a1afd6c0895e330"},{"id":"la_batch21_polynomial_greatest_common_divisor_015","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Find the monic GCD of $x^3-x$ and $x^2-1$.","options":["$x^2-1$","$1$","$x$","$x-1$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"d1079209200059c244f0b79f"},{"id":"la_batch21_polynomial_greatest_common_divisor_016","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"The Euclidean algorithm gives remainders $r_1=x^2+1$, $r_2=2x-2$, $r_3=0$. What is the monic GCD?","options":["$0$","$x-1$","$x^2+1$","$2x-2$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"817a47a05cda8b6058ffd637"},{"id":"la_batch21_polynomial_greatest_common_divisor_017","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why does the Euclidean algorithm eventually stop over $\\mathbb F[x]$?","options":["The coefficients eventually become integers","Every polynomial eventually becomes linear after adding roots","The quotient degrees strictly increase forever","The nonzero remainder degrees strictly decrease"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"cb0b01e05de0f132a6391fcf"},{"id":"la_batch21_polynomial_greatest_common_divisor_018","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What information does a nonconstant GCD give about two polynomials?","options":["They share a nontrivial polynomial factor","They have no common roots in any field","They must have the same degree","They must be scalar multiples"],"answer_